\begin{rem}\label{gk-result}
There exist a constant $0<C<\infty$ such that
$$\limsup_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{g_k(n)}{\log^2 n}=C~~~~a.s.$$
The key idea is to adapt Lemma~\ref{lem1.6} by redefining
\begin{equation*}
    g_{k}(s):=\#\mathcal{G}_{s,k}=\#\left\{ x :  \xi(x,\tau^G_s)=k\right\}
\end{equation*}
which counts the number of sites visited exactly $k$ times by $(G_n)$ at time $\tau^G_s$. Under the assumptions of Lemma~\ref{prop1.5}, a similar bound holds,
\begin{equation*}
    \left(\frac{(1-\epsilon)\log s}{2}\right)^j\leq \e \left[\binom{g_{k}(s)}{j}\right]\leq \left(\frac{(1+\epsilon)\log s}{2}\right)^j.
\end{equation*}
The remainder of the proof follows similarly to \cite{Ma88}.
The definition of $g_k$ (sites visited exactly $k$ times) creates correlations between different parts of the path, unlike the "point of increase." This inherent correlation prevents a decomposition into i.i.d.~excursions.
\end{rem}

