post_theorem_intro_text:
For $\sigma > 1,$ we can write
$$
    Z(s) = Z(s+1)\prod_{P}(1-N(P)^{-s}).
$$
Theorem~\ref{thm:moments} will follow by considering the Ruelle zeta function, which is defined as
\begin{align}\label{ruelle}
Z_1(s)=Z(s)/Z(s+1)=\prod_{P}(1-N(P)^{-s}).
\end{align}
This function has a meromorphic continuation to $\mathbb{C}$.
For $\sigma > 1,$ we can write
$$
    Z_1(s) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{b'_n}{x^s_n}, \ \frac{1}{Z_1(s)} = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{c'_n}{x^s_n},
$$
where $1=x_1 < x_2 < \dots$ and $b'_n, c'_n \in \mathbb{Z}.$ Note that the absolute value of $b'_n$ is bounded by how many expressions of the form
$$
    N(P_1) \dots N(P_K)
$$
the number $x_n$ has. While $c'_n$ is positive and equal to how many expressions of the form
$$
    N(P_1)^{\alpha_1} \dots N(P_K)^{\alpha_K},
$$
$\alpha_k \in \mathbb{N},$ the number $x_n$ has. Thus we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ineq}
    |b'_n| \le c'_n .
\end{equation}
In \cite{drungilas2021}, the conditional version of Theorem \ref{thm:moments} was proved for $\Gamma=\text{PSL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ assuming the conditions for coefficients
\begin{align*}
\sum_{x_n\le x} b'_n, \sum_{x_n\le x} c'_n-\rho x \ll x\exp\left(-45\frac{\log x}{\log\log x}\right),
\end{align*}
where $\rho > 0$ is the residue of $Z_1^{-1}(s)$ at $s=1$, or the separation condition 
\begin{align}\label{star}
x_{n+1}-{x_n} \gg\exp\left(-\exp\left(c\sqrt{\log x_n\log\log x_n}\right)\right)
\end{align}
are valid for some specific $c>0$. The separation condition \eqref{star} was inherited from Landau \cite[Chapter 75]{landau1909}, where he investigated the second moment for the general Dirichlet series.

The function $Z_1$ is a Beurling zeta-function (see also \cite[Ending notes]{dgn2019} and \cite[Introduction]{drungilas2021}). Notably,  the proof that the second moment of $Z_1(1+it)$ exists does not rely on specific properties of the Selberg zeta-function, aside from the prime geodesic theorem \eqref{eq:primegeodesictheorem} with $\theta<1$. Therefore, we will derive the moment of $Z_1(1+it)$ from the following two general propositions, which examine the cases of the Beurling zeta-function and general Dirichlet series.

We consider generalized prime numbers systems $\mathcal P$, often called $g$-primes,  
$$
1<p_1\le p_2\le\dots,
$$
with $p_j\in\mathbb R$ tending to infinity and the associated Beurling zeta-function
\begin{align}\label{eulerbeurling}
\zeta_{\mathcal P}(s)=\prod_{j=1}^{\infty}\left(1-\frac1{p_j^s}\right)^{-1}.
\end{align}
A formal multiplication of the product gives the Dirichlet series
\begin{align}\label{dirichletbeurling}
\zeta_{\mathcal P}(s)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{d_n}{\nu_n^s},
\end{align}
where $1=\nu_1<\nu_2<\nu_3<\dots$ is the increasing sequence of  power-products of $g$-primes with corresponding multiplicities $1=d_1, d_2, d_3, \dots$. Similarly, there are integers $1=e_1, e_2, e_3, \dots,$ such that 
\begin{align}\label{reciprocalbeurling}
\frac1{\zeta_{\mathcal P}(s)}=\prod_{j=1}^{\infty}\left(1-\frac1{p_j^s}\right)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{e_n}{\nu_n^s}.
\end{align}
As in \eqref{eq:ineq} we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ineq1}
    |e_n| \le d_n .
\end{equation}
In this paper, we always assume that there is a fixed $0<\alpha<1$, such that
\begin{align}\label{pxli}
\pi_{\mathcal P}(x)=\sum_{p_n\le x}1=\operatorname{Li}(x)+O(x^\alpha).
\end{align}
Then Hilberdink and Lapidus \cite[Theorem 2.1]{hilberdink2006}  obtained that $\zeta_{\mathcal P}(s)$ has an analytic continuation to the half-plane $\Re s>\alpha$ except for a simple pole at $s=1$ and $\zeta_{\mathcal P}(s)\ne0$ in this region,
With these considerations, we formulate the following proposition.

\begin{Proposition}\label{thm:moment1}
Under the assumption \eqref{pxli}, we have that    \begin{equation}\label{eq:momentzeta1}
\lim_{T \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_{1}^T|\zeta_{\mathcal P}(1+it)|^2dt = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{d^2_n}{\nu_n^2}
    \end{equation}
    and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:momentzetainverse1}
\lim_{T \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_{1}^T|\zeta_{\mathcal P}(1+it)|^{-2}dt = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{e^2_n}{\nu_n^2}.
    \end{equation}
    Both series on the RHS converge.
\end{Proposition}
 Moreover in \cite[Theorem 2.2]{hilberdink2006} it is proved that
\begin{align}\label{betax}
    \sum_{x_n \le x}d_n = \eta x + D(x),
\end{align}
where $\eta > 0$ is a constant and 
\begin{align}\label{Dx}
D(x)\ll x\exp\left(-c\sqrt{\log x \log\log x}\right),
\end{align}
with $c > 0$ fixed. Note that Broucke, Debruyne, and Vindas \cite{bdv20} constructed a Beurling generalized number system which satisfies \eqref{pxli} with $\alpha=1/2$ and \eqref{betax} with $
D(x)=\Omega\left( x\exp\left(-C\sqrt{\log x \log\log x}\right)\right)
$ for some $C>0$. Proposition \ref{thm:moment1} allows to remove the separation condition of the type \eqref{star} in Theorem 1 in \cite{drungilas2021}, where the second moment of $\zeta_{\mathcal P}^{\pm1}(1+it)$ was treated.

Proposition \ref{thm:moment1} will follow from a statement for general Dirichlet series. 
Denote
$$
    f(s) = \sum_{j=1}^\infty \frac{a_j}{n_j^s},
$$
where $(n_j)$ is a strictly increasing sequence of positive reals which tends to infinity and $a_j > 0$. Then the second moment of $f(s)$ at $\sigma = 1$ exists even for a weaker bound than in  \eqref{Dx}.
\begin{Proposition}\label{thm:moment2}
Let $a_j > 0$, $\varepsilon>0$ and assume that
$$
A(x)= \sum_{n_j \le x} a_j=\varrho x + R(x),
$$
where $\varrho > 0$ is a constant and 
\begin{align}\label{eq:Rxbound}
R(x)\ll \frac{x}{(\log x)^{3/2+\varepsilon}}.
\end{align}
Then the sum defining $f(s)$ converges for $\sigma > 1$ and the function has a continuous extension to $\sigma\ge1$,   $s\ne1$, and
\begin{align}\label{mainmoment}
\lim_{T \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_{1}^T|f(1+it)|^2dt = \sum_{j=1}^\infty \frac{a^2_j}{n^2_j}.
   \end{align}
    The series on the RHS converges.
\end{Proposition}

Proposition \ref{thm:moment2} extends the recent result of Broucke and Hilberdink \cite{bh}, where they obtained that the second moment of $f(s)$ exists for $\sigma>(1+\eta)/2$, if $\sum_{n_j \le x} a_j=\varrho x + O(x^{\eta})$ for some fixed $\varrho>0$ and $\eta<1$. The significant feature of their approach is that they do not require any separation condition for $n_j$ in the proof.  

We establish Proposition \ref{thm:moment2} in the second section. The third section is dedicated to proving Theorem \ref{thm:moments} and Proposition \ref{thm:moment1}.

We use notation $f(x) \ll g(x)$ or $f(x) = O(g(x))$ to mean that $|f(x)| \le c|g(x)|$ for some constant $c > 0,$ when $x$ is big enough. Additionally, we use  $f(x) \prec g(x)$ or $f(x)=o(g(x))$ to signify that $f(x)/g(x) \rightarrow 0,$ when $x \rightarrow \infty.$ The notation $f(x)=\Omega(g(x))$ means that exists a constant $C$ such that the inequality $|f(x)| > C|g(x)|$ holds infinitely often for arbitrarily large values of $x$.

