\paragraph{Proof overview.} The proof is "by way of contradiction": assuming "a graph has high rankwidth but low min-bal-cutrank," so that "every part of the graph can be cleanly separated with a low-rank cut." The argument introduces the following definition.

\begin{definition}[Rank-tangle of order $k$] \label{def: rank-tangle of order k}
Following Robertson-Seymour \cite{ROBERTSON1991153}, a rank-tangle of order $k$ is a set $\mathcal{T}$ of subsets of $V(G)$ such that \begin{enumerate}\item For every partition $(X,Y)$ of $V(G)$ with $\operatorname{cutrank}_G(X,Y)<k$, exactly one of $X$ or $Y$ is in $\mathcal{T}$ (and is called the ``small side'')\item No three chosen small sides have union $V(G)$; i.e., for any sets $S_1, S_2, S_3 \in \mathcal{T}$, their union $S_1 \cup S_2 \cup S_3 \neq V(G)$.\end{enumerate}
The existence of a rank-tangle of order $k$ witnesses that $\mathrm{rankwidth}(G)\geq k$.
\end{definition}

This tangle points to the `more connected' component of a separation, and we use it to find a separation $(X,Y)$ such that the tangle-selected component is maximally connected. With $X$ chosen "maximal" (the "small side"), one then "compress[es] $X$ by selecting a subset of representative vertices," and shows that "finding a low-rank balanced cut in this compressed graph would allow us to extend $X$, which contradicts its maximality." 

The proof proceeds in three stages: (1) We use the following claim to extract a low-rank separation $(X,Y)$ where $X$ is "maximally large" and "well-behaved," meaning "$X$ is robustly connected to $Y$" ("no small component of $X$ can be separated from the rest of the graph with a low-rank cut").

\begin{claim}
\label{claim:rank-tangle existence}
    There exists a partition $(X,Y)$ of $V(G)$ such that $\operatorname{cutrank}(X,Y) < 70r$, the rank-tangle $\tau$ of order $72r$ orients this partition towards $X$ (i.e., $X \in \tau$), and $(X,Y)$ is $(3|Y|, 1/7)$-well-behaved. Moreover, $|X|$ is maximum among all separations oriented by $\tau$ with $\mathrm{cutrank}<70r$ (and, subject to this, $\mathrm{cutrank}(X,Y)$ is minimum).
\end{claim}

(2) We bound "the number of distinct neighborhood patterns that vertices in $Y$ can form within $X$" and thereby find "a small subset of representative vertices in $X$"; concretely, we "compress $X$ by taking one representative per $Y$-neighborhood class" using the following claim, which preserves $\operatorname{cutrank}(X,Y)$.

\begin{claim}
\label{claim:findingH}
    There exists an induced subgraph $H\subseteq G$ which is obtained from $G$ by deleting some vertices from $X$ such that for  all $v\in X$, there exists at least one vertex $v'\in X\cap V(H) \eqqcolon X^*$ such that $N(v)\cap Y = N(v')\cap Y$.
\end{claim}

(3) We then use the following lemma to form an induced subgraph $H'$ so that "any balanced cut of $H'$ of rank at most $r$" forces a split of $Y$ and gives "two lifted separations of $G$ whose cutranks are at most $72r$"; combined with the tangle axiom, this yields a strongly-connected side strictly larger than $X$, contradicting maximality.

\begin{lemma}[Splitting $Y$]
    \label{lemma: splitting Y reduction}
    There exists an induced subgraph $H'\subseteq H$ and a balanced partition $(A', B')$ of $H'$ such that $A' \cap Y \neq \emptyset$ and $B'\cap Y\neq\emptyset$, $\operatorname{cutrank}_{H'}(A', B') < r$, and if $X^{**} \eqqcolon V(H') \cap X^*$, then \[\operatorname{cutrank}_{H'}(X^{**}, Y) \leq \operatorname{cutrank}_H(X^*, Y) = \operatorname{cutrank}_G(X,Y) < 70r.\]
\end{lemma}

This contradiction completes the proof of the following theorem.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{MainThm}
\label{thm:main}
Let $r\ge1$ and $G=(V, E)$ be a graph. If $\mathrm{rankwidth}(G)\ge 72r$, then $G$ contains an induced subgraph $H$ with $\operatorname{min-bal-cutrank(H)}\ge r$.
\end{restatable}

