expanded_sketch:
To prove Theorem~\ref{thm:main-theorem}, the paper first splits into the cases where $X$ is hyperbolic and where $X$ is not hyperbolic. The hyperbolic case follows from the convergence group theorem (with some care if $X$ is hyperbolic but not a Cayley graph).

The focus is then on the non-hyperbolic case, where the stated goal is to show that “non-hyperbolicity plus
\begin{equation}
  \tag{$\rm{UBQ}$}\label{eq:BQ}
  \parbox{4in}{    \strut
    There exists $\sigma > 0$ such that for any connected, bi-infinite quasi-geodesic $\rho \subset X$, we have that $X \setminus N_\sigma(\rho)$ contains exactly two wide connected components. 
    \strut
  }
\end{equation}
 implies that $X$ has quadratic growth.” From quadratic growth, Trofimov’s theorem (building on Gromov) together with the Bass--Guivarc’h formula is used to conclude that $X$ is quasi-isometric to the Euclidean plane.

The argument proceeds via “witnesses” (pairs of rays probing deep into the two wide components bounded by a bi-infinite quasi-geodesic), and a key tool is the “witness protection lemma,” asserting that bounded perturbations of a bi-infinite geodesic preserve the ability of the original witnesses to witness “two distinct deep components” for the new quasi-geodesic. Next, the paper constructs a large figure called a “cross-examiner”; by attaching paths to it and applying the witness protection lemma, it constructs closed loops that “coarsely bound regions of $X$ in a controlled way,” compared to Jordan curves. Combined with an inequality of Varopoulos, this yields (later) a criterion for showing $X$ has quadratic growth.

Finally, the paper studies separation properties of “quasi-circles” and “truncated quasi-circles,” proving a dichotomy: either all (truncated) quasi-circles coarsely bound regions of uniformly bounded depth (“limited jurisdiction”), or $X$ has quadratic growth. Later, Arzel\`a--Ascoli is applied to build a sequence of nested truncated quasi-circles along a bi-infinite quasi-geodesic; this nesting, together with the witness protection lemma, shows truncated quasi-circles “cannot possibly have limited jurisdiction,” forcing quadratic growth and completing the proof.

