\label{theo:main}
There exists a pair $(\varepsilon_0,{\alpha_{0}})$ of positive constants that depend on the mass and energy of $F_{\mathrm{in}}^\varepsilon$, $m$, and $q$ introduced in~\eqref{eq:defE}, such that for $\varepsilon\in(0,\e_0)$ and if
$$\|h_{\mathrm{in}}^\varepsilon\|_{ \mathcal{E}} \leqslant {\alpha_{0}},$$
then, the equation \eqref{eq:heps} has a unique solution $h_{\varepsilon} \in\mathcal{C}\big([0,\infty); \mathcal{E}\big) \cap L^1_{\rm{loc}}\big((0,\infty);\E_1\big)$ 
satisfying 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:heZg}
\left\|h_\varepsilon(t)\right\|_{ \mathcal{E}}\leqslant \frac{C ({\alpha_{0}})}{\sqrt{1+t}}\,,
\qquad \forall \, t \geqslant 0\,, \qquad \forall \, \varepsilon \in (0,\e_0)\,,
\end{equation}
for some positive constant $C({ \alpha_{0}}) >0$ independent of $\varepsilon$. Moreover, the  solution splits as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:heps0+1}
h_{\varepsilon}(t)=\ho_{\varepsilon}(t)+\hu_{\varepsilon}(t)\end{equation}
and, for any $T >0$ there exists $\bm{h}\in L^{2}\left((0,T);\mathcal{H}\right)$ such that, up to extraction of a subsequence, it holds that
	\begin{equation}\begin{cases}\label{eq:mode-conv}
	\left\{\ho_{\varepsilon}\right\}_{\varepsilon} \text{converges to $0$ strongly  in } L^{1}((0,T)\,;{\E_1})\,, \\[0.2cm]
	\left\{\hu_{\varepsilon}\right\}_{\varepsilon} \text{converges to $\bm{h}$ weakly in } L^{2}\left((0,T)\,;\mathcal{H}\right)\,.
	\end{cases}\end{equation}
In particular, there exist 
	\begin{align*}
	\varrho \in L^{2}&\left((0,T)\,;\mathbb{W}^{m,2}_{x}(\mathbb{T}^{d})\right)\,, \qquad
	u \in L^{2}\left((0,T)\,;\left(\mathbb{W}^{m,2}_{x}(\mathbb{T}^{d})\right)^{d}\right)\,,  \\
	&\qquad\text{and} \qquad \vartheta \in L^{2}\left((0,T)\,;\mathbb{W}^{m,2}_x(\mathbb{T}^{d})\right)\,, 
	\end{align*}
such that
	\begin{equation}\label{def:bmh}
	\bm{h}(t,x,v)=\left(\varrho(t,x)+u(t,x)\cdot v + \frac{1}{2}\vartheta(t,x)(|v|^{2}-3\en_{\star})\right)\mathcal M(v)
	\end{equation}
where $\mathcal M$ is the Maxwellian distribution \eqref{eq:max} and $(\varrho,u,\vartheta)=(\varrho(t,x),u(t,x),\vartheta(t,x))$ are suitable solutions to the following incompressible Navier-Stokes-Fourier system with forcing term
\begin{equation}\label{eq:NSFint}
\begin{cases}
\partial_{t}u-\frac{\nu_{0}}{\en_{\star}}\,\Delta_{x}u + {\en_{\star}}u\cdot \nabla_{x}\,u+\nabla_{x}p= \xi(t)\,u\,,\\[6pt]
\partial_{t}\,\vartheta-\dfrac{\nu_{1}}{\en_{\star}^{2}}\,\Delta_{x}\vartheta + \en_{\star}\,u\cdot \nabla_{x}\vartheta
	=3\frac{1-\gamma}{2}\,\en_{\star}^{2}\,\xi(t)\,\vartheta\,,\\[8pt]
\mathrm{div}_{x}u=0\,, \qquad \varrho + \en_{\star}\,\vartheta = 0\,,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
subject to initial conditions $(\varrho_{\mathrm{in}},u_{\mathrm{in}},\vE_{\mathrm{in}})$, entirely determined by the limiting behaviour of $h_{\rm in}^{\varepsilon}$ as~$†\varepsilon \to 0$. The viscosity $\nu_0 >0$ and heat conductivity $\nu_1 >0$ as well as the forcing coefficient $\xi(t)$ are explicit.

