 Note that the invariance of the metric implies that it is geodesically complete, and so the Hopf--Rinow theorem implies that it is proper (closed and bounded sets are compact). Then one obtains a metric on $X = G/\Gamma$ by setting
\begin{equation*}
 \dist_X(g_1\Gamma, g_2\Gamma) := \inf\big\{\dist_G(g_1\gamma, g_2): \gamma \in \Gamma \big\}.
