The notions of almost containment and commensurability are fundamental in the study of groups definable in finite-dimensional theories.
\begin{defn}
\begin{itemize}
    \item  Let $G$ be a group and $A, B\leq G$ subgroups. $A$ \emph{almost contains} $B$, denoted $A\apprge B$, if $|B:B\cap A|$ is finite.
    \item  If $A\apprle B$ and $B\apprle A$, $A$ and $B$ are \emph{commensurable}.
    \item Given a family of subgroups $\{G_i\}_{i\in I}$ in $G$, the family $\{G_i\}_{i\in I}$ is \emph{uniformly commensurable} if there exists $n<\omega$ such that $|G_i/G_i\cap G_j|\leq n$ for every $i,j\in I$.
\end{itemize}
