Note that for $r = 1,$ 
\begin{align*}
c_1 + \ell_1 &= (a + d) + (-(a + d)) \\
&= a + d - a - d \\
&= 0.
\end{align*}
Now, for $r = 2,$ we will show the the identity $c_2 + c_1\ell_1 + 2\ell_2 = 0.$
\begin{align*}
c_2 + c_1\ell_1 + 2\ell_2 &= (a^2 + d^2 + 2bc) + (a+d)(-(a+d)) + 2(ad - bc) \\
&= a^2 + d^2 + 2bc - (a^2 + 2ad + d^2) + 2ad - 2bc \\
&= a^2 + d^2 + 2bc - a^2 - 2ad - d^2 + 2ad - 2bc \\
&= 0 .
\end{align*}

Now, we consider that $r=3>n=2.$ Here we will check that $c_3 + c_2\ell_1 + c_1\ell_2 = 0.$ For that first, we have to compute $c_3.$ There are 8 distinct closed walks of length 3, shown in Figure \ref{fig:walks-3}:
