\label{TH-00}
Let $u$ be a suitable weak solutions. Suppose that 
the following two conditions hold:
\begin{equation}
\liminf_{r\to 0}M(r):=\liminf_{r\to 0}\int_{B_r}\!\! \left( \frac{|u|^2}{r^3} + \frac{|\nabla u|^2}{r} \right) <\infty, \quad \liminf_{r\to 0}\frac1{r^2}\int_{B_r}\!\!(|u|^2+2P)u\cdot\frac x{|x|}>0.
\end{equation}
Then  for any $\{r_k\}_{k=1}^\infty, r_k\downarrow 0$ there is a subsequence $r_{k_m}$ such that 
the scaled solutions $u_{r_{k_m}}(x)=r_{k_m}u(r_{k_m}x)$, converge to 
a homogenous vector field of degree negative one, and hence 
$x=0$ is a regular point.

