pre_theorem_intro_text:
Let $M$ be a compact Riemannian manifold of dimension $d$. Let $\Delta$ be the Laplace--Beltrami operator on $M$, and let $f$ be its eigenfunctions such that $\Delta f=-N^2f$, $N>1$. The study of concentration of $f$ has become a popular subject \cite{Zel17}, and one of the approaches is to bound its restriction to submanifolds of $M$, as initiated by Tataru \cite{Tat98}  and Reznikov \cite{Rez04}. Let $S$ be a submanifold of dimension $k$. Let $L^p(M)$, $L^p(S)$ be the Lebesgue spaces associated to the Riemannian volume measure on $M$ and $S$ respectively. The following fundamental restriction bounds for general $M$ were established by Burq--G\'erard--Tzvetkov \cite{BGT07} and Hu \cite{Hu09}: it holds
\begin{align}\label{eq: BGT}
    \|f\|_{L^p(S)}\lesssim N^{\rho(k,d,p)}\|f\|_{L^2(M)},
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align*}
\rho(d-1,d,p)&=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
\frac{d-1}{2}-\frac{d-1}{p}, \qquad \text{if }\frac{2d}{d-1}\leq p\leq\infty,\\
\frac{d-1}{4}-\frac{d-2}{2p}, \qquad \text{if }2\leq p\leq \frac{2d}{d-1},
\end{array}\right.\\
\rho(d-2,d,p)&=\frac{d-1}{2}-\frac{d-2}{p},  \qquad  \text{if }2<p\leq \infty, \\
