(in establishing the main theorem) for solutions to the problem
\begin{split}
&\partial_tv-\nu\Delta v+v\cdot\nabla v+\nabla p=f,\cr
&\divv v=0\quad {\rm in}\ \ \Omega^T,\cr
\end{split}
\label{1.1}
\end{equation}
under the axisymmetry constraint, where $\Omega^T:=\Omega\times(0,T)$, $T>0$, \\$v=v(x,t)\in\R^3$ denotes the velocity field, $p=p(x,t)\in\R$ denotes the pressure function, $f=f(x,t)\in\R^3$ denotes the external force field, $\nu>0$ denotes the viscosity, and $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ denotes the Cartesian coordinates. As for $\Omega$ we focus on the case of a finite cylinder,
$$
\Omega=\{x\in\R^3\colon x_1^2+x_2^2<R^2,|x_3|<a\},
$$
where $a$, $R>0$ are constants. We note that
$$
S:=\partial\Omega=S_1\cup S_2,
$$
where
\[
\begin{split}
&S_1=\{x\in\R^3\colon\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2}=R,\ x_3\in[-a,a]\},\\
&S_2=\{x\in\R^3\colon\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2}<R,\ x_3\in\{-a,a\}\}
\end{split}
\]
with the initial condition
\begin{equation}
v|_{t=0}=v_0,
\label{1.3}
\end{equation}
one needs the expansions
\begin{aligned}
    &v_r(r,z,t)=a_1(z,t)r+a_2(z,t)r^3+\dots,\cr
&v_\varphi(r,z,t)=b_1(z,t)r+b_2(z,t)r^3+\dots,\cr
&\psi(r,z,t)=d_1(z,t)r+d_2(z,t)r^3+\dots,\cr
&\psi_1(r,z,t)=d_1(z,t)+d_2(z,t)r^2+\dots,\cr
&\psi_{1,r}(r,z,t)=2d_2(z,t)r+\dots,.\cr
\end{aligned}
\label{1.21}
\end{equation}
so “sufficiently regular solutions must be considered.” Using results of Ladyzhenshaya \cite{L1} and the partial regularity theory of Caffarelli–Kohn–Nirenberg \cite{CKN}, any singularity of axisymmetric solutions “must occur on the axis of symmetry only.” The method therefore “make[s] use of regular solutions for which there is no singularities at the axis of symmetry,” ensuring that the expansions above are valid, and thereby enabling control of $X(t)$ leading to the estimate above. It is noted as an open problem whether one can control $X(t)$ “without exploiting these expansions.”

