The post-theorem introduction gives the following proof idea for the main theorem:

- The “key idea” is to “make full use of the a priori $L^\infty$ bound on the hydrostatic vorticity $\omega$,” namely
\begin{equation}\label{eq:omegaMP}
\|\omega(t)\|_{L^{\infty}} \leq\|\omega_{0}\|_{L^{\infty}}.
\end{equation}
- This $L^\infty$ bound “serves as the foundation for deriving a hierarchy of enhanced a priori energy estimates,” which “significantly strengthen the regularity control of the solution.”
- The authors “develop an iterative procedure to obtain successive a priori bounds at higher levels of regularity.”
- “As a result,” they “relax the global well-posedness assumption from $\alpha \geq \tfrac{6}{5}$ to the improved threshold $\alpha \geq \alpha_0$,” thereby “extending the range of admissible dissipation exponents.”

Additionally, the remark explains the uniqueness mechanism: the main theorem “establishes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BKM}	
\int_0^T\|\Lambda_{h}^{\frac{3-\alpha}{2}}\omega(t)\|_{L^2}^2\dd t<\infty,
\end{equation}
and therefore guarantees uniqueness of the strong solution,” since a “sufficient condition ensuring uniqueness is the regularity criterion given by the equation above.”

