ref_dict.Weaker ineq:
\begin{thm}\label{Weaker ineq}Let $\Omega$ be a bounded convex domain in $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then we have
\begin{align*}
    \mu_{k+1}(\Omega)\lesssim n^7\mu_k(\Omega)
\end{align*}for any natural number $k$.
\begin{proof}Proposition \ref{Hacher lem} gives the existence of a rectangular domain $R$ such that
      \begin{align*}
     \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}R\subseteq  \Omega\subseteq nR.
    \end{align*}Let $\widetilde{R}$ be the flat torus whose fundamental domain is $R$. Observe that the $k$-the eigenvalue $\lambda_k(\widetilde{R})$ of the Laplacian on $\widetilde{R}$ is equal to $\mu_k(R)$ (\cite[Section II.2]{Ch}). Also since $\widetilde{R}$ is a homogeneous manifold, we use P.~Li's theorem (\cite[Theorem 11]{Li}) to get $\lambda_{k+1}(\widetilde{R})\lesssim \lambda_k(\widetilde{R})$, yielding that 
    \begin{align*}
        \mu_{k+1}(R)\lesssim \mu_k(R).
    \end{align*}Applying Theorem \ref{domain monotonicity} twice we thus obtain
    \begin{align*}
        \mu_{k+1}(\Omega)\lesssim n^2\mu_{k+1}\Big(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}R\Big)=n^3\mu_{k+1}(R)\lesssim n^3\mu_k(R)=\ &n^5\mu_k(n R)\\ \lesssim \ & n^7\mu_k(\Omega).
    \end{align*}This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\end{thm}

pre_theorem_intro_text:
Let $\Omega$ be a bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^n$ with Lipschitz boundary. We consider the following Neumann eigenvalue problem of the Laplacian: 
\begin{equation}\label{Neumann eigenvalue problem}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u = \mu u \quad \text{in } \Omega,\\
\partial_n u=0\quad \text{on }\partial \Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\partial_n$ denotes the directional derivative with respect to $n$, the outward unit normal vector to $\partial\Omega$.
In this situation it is known the compactness of the embedding $H^1(\Omega)$ to $L^2(\Omega)$ and it gives that the eigenvalues of (\ref{Neumann eigenvalue problem}) can be listed with multiplicity as follows: 
\begin{align*}
     0=\mu_0(\Omega)<\mu_1(\Omega)\le\mu_2(\Omega)\le \dots  \le \mu_k(\Omega)\le \mu_{k+1}(\Omega)\le \dots \to\infty.
\end{align*}

Our main concern in this paper is the relation between those eigenvalues. One of the main theorems in this paper is the following. We denote by $\alpha\lesssim \beta$ if $\alpha\leq c\beta$ for some universal concrete constant $c>0$ (which means $c$ does not depend on any parameters such as dimension and $k$ etc).

