\begin{theorem}\label{thm: jac}
Assume that $0\leq\beta\leq \alpha$. For $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$, let $T_{n}^{\alpha,\beta}$
   denote the convolution operator on $\mathbb{T}=\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}$ with kernel $P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(\cos\theta)$, 
   where $\theta\in \mathbb{T}$.  
Then for the following cases:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\alpha > \frac{1}{2}$,
    \item $0 \le \alpha < \frac{1}{2}$,
    \item $\alpha = \beta = \frac{1}{2}$,
\end{enumerate}
the operator $T_n^{\alpha,\beta}$ satisfies
\begin{align}\label{eq: Tn sharp bound}
    \|T_n^{\alpha,\beta}\|_{L^{p'}(\mathbb{T}) \to L^p(\mathbb{T})} \lesssim A(\alpha, p/2, n), \quad \text{for all } p \ge 2.
\end{align}

\end{theorem}

In the presence of three-dimensional factors (the case $\alpha=\beta=\tfrac12$), another key input is “the positivity of Fourier coefficients of spherical functions,” namely: for $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$,
\begin{lemma}[Proposition 9.4 of Chapter III of \cite{Hel08}]\label{lem: Phi_n=sum}
For $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$, we have 
    $$\Phi_n(\theta)=\sum_{j=0}^{q_n} c_{n,j} e^{im_{n,j}\theta},\qquad \theta\in\mathbb{T},$$
    where $c_{n,j}\geq 0$ and $m_{n,j}\in\mathbb{Z}$. In particular, $\sum_{j=0}^{q_n} c_{n,j}=\Phi_n(0)=1$. 
\end{lemma}
This “helps preserve oscillation in the convolution kernel and effectively reduces the study of arbitrary submanifolds to those of product type; see”
\begin{align}\label{eq: K=sum}
    \mathscr{K}(\underline{\theta}',\underline{\theta})=&\left(\prod_{i\notin\{i_1,\ldots,i_k\}}\sum_{j_i=0}^{q_{i,n_i}}c_{i,n_i,j_i}e^{im_{i,n_i,j_i}(\theta_i(\underline{\theta})-\theta_i(\underline{\theta}'))}\right) \widetilde{\mathscr{K}}(\underline{\theta}',\underline{\theta}),
\end{align}

The paper’s proof structure is: we prove the estimates in the main theorem by first establishing restriction estimates for the joint eigenfunctions of the Laplace--Beltrami operators on each factor $M_i$, using the preceding theorem. Then the preceding theorem is proved separately, and finally sharpness of
\begin{align}\label{eq: no loss}
    \|f\|_{L^p(S)}\lesssim N^{\frac{d-2}{2}-\frac{k}{p}}\|f\|_{L^2(M)}, \qquad\text{for all }p\geq 2, 
\end{align}
is shown later.

