To prove the main theorem, the authors "introduce the notion of an \emph{allocation}, which is a coarse filling of a Young diagram adhering to certain constraints." Rather than choosing a symbol for each cell, they "subdivide the Young diagram into sub-rectangles and partition the symbol set into smaller subsets," and an allocation "specifies how many elements of each symbol subset should be in each sub-rectangle." Using Hilton's outline-rectangle framework, they "rely on Hilton's result, which states that any outline rectangle can be traced back to a Latin square, to demonstrate that if a Young diagram has an allocation, then it has a Latin filling." Therefore, to prove the main theorem, they reduce it to proving that wide diagrams with three distinct row lengths admit allocations: \begin{conjecture}\label{conj:wide_implies_outline}
    Any wide Young diagram has an allocation.
\end{conjecture} They prove this conjecture for Young diagrams with three distinct row lengths, i.e., show such a diagram "has an allocation," which then implies it has a Latin filling.

