theorem.content:
\label{thm:main}
There are no separable universal objects for the categories of separable Banach algebras, in either the commutative or the general (not-necessarily commutative) setting, for contractive or merely bounded homomorphic embeddings. More precisely,
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(A)] There is no separable \emph{commutative} Banach algebra \(B\) such that every separable \emph{commutative} Banach algebra \(A\) admits a \emph{contractive} homomorphic embedding \(A\hookrightarrow B\).
  \item[(B)] There is no separable \emph{commutative} Banach algebra \(B\) such that every separable \emph{commutative} Banach algebra \(A\) admits a (bounded) homomorphic embedding \(A\hookrightarrow B\).
  \item[(C)] There is no separable Banach algebra \(B\) such that every separable Banach algebra \(A\) admits a \emph{contractive} homomorphic embedding \(A\hookrightarrow B\).
  \item[(D)] There is no separable Banach algebra \(B\) such that every separable Banach algebra \(A\) admits a (bounded) homomorphic embedding \(A\hookrightarrow B\).
\end{enumerate}

full_context:
For commutative C*-algebras, the situation is well understood. Since every compact metrisable space is a quotient of the Cantor set $\Delta := 2^{\mathbb{N}}$, the algebra $C(\Delta)$ of continuous functions on the Cantor set is universal for separable commutative C*-algebras: every such algebra embeds isometrically as a subalgebra of $C(\Delta)$. In contrast, there is \emph{no} separable C*-algebra that is universal for \emph{all} separable C*-algebras (commutative or not), as can be deduced from \cite[Proposition 2.6]{JungePisier:1995}.

For general Banach algebras without additional structure, it is folklore that there exists no separable commutative Banach algebra $B$ such that every separable commutative Banach algebra admits an \emph{isometric} embedding into $B$. A straightforward proof appears in \cite{Kania:2012}: if $p$ and $q$ are commuting projections in a Banach algebra, then $\|p - q\| \geqslant 1$, so any set of commuting projections is discrete; since projections in Banach algebras can have arbitrarily large norms, a commutative Banach algebra containing uncountably many commuting projections must be non-separable.

However, the question of universality for \emph{bounded} (or \emph{contractive}) \emph{homomorphic embeddings}, \emph{i.e.}, embeddings that are merely required to have closed range, appears to have been left open in the literature. This is the gap we address in the present work.

We work over the field of complex numbers however the proofs are valid for real Banach algebras too. A \emph{homomorphism} between Banach algebras means a bounded algebra homomorphism. An \emph{embedding} is an injective homomorphism that is a topological isomorphism onto its (closed) range. Sometimes we additionally require homomorphisms to be contractive; in the other we do not, however we always stress this out explicitly.

Our argument is unified and uses only the ordinary projective tensor product. Given a bounded bilinear form \(\beta\), we build a separable test algebra \(A(\beta)\) whose multiplication remembers \(\beta\). Any homomorphic embedding \(\phi:A(\beta)\to B\) forces the linearised form \(\widetilde{\beta}\) to factor through the fixed separable space \(G:=B\widehat{\otimes}_{\!\pi} B\). Choosing \(\beta\) so that \(\widetilde{\beta}\) (equivalently, a composition \(S\circ\widetilde{\beta}_{U}\) defined below) does not factor through \(G\), by Johnson--Szankowski \cite{JS2009}, yields a contradiction. In the commutative case we pick \(\beta\) symmetric so that \(A(\beta)\) itself is commutative, but we still linearise via \(X\widehat{\otimes}_{\!\pi} X\) and avoid radicals and symmetric tensor products entirely.

We work over \(\mathbb{C}\) however the results remain valid for real Banach algebras too. A \emph{homomorphism} between Banach algebras means a bounded algebra homomorphism. An \emph{embedding} is an injective homomorphism that is a topological isomorphism onto its closed range.

\title[There is no universal separable Banach algebra]{There is no universal separable Banach algebra}
\author{Tomasz Kania}
\date{\today}
\dedicatory{Dedicated to W.~B.~Johnson on the occasion of his 80\textsuperscript{th} birthday}
\subjclass[2020]{46H10, 46H15, 46M05}
\keywords{Banach algebra, projective tensor product, factorisation of operators, universal Banach algebra, Johnson--Szankowski theorem}
\thanks{RVO: 67985840}
\begin{abstract}
We prove that no separable Banach algebra is universal for homomorphic embeddings of all separable Banach algebras, whether embeddings are merely bounded or required to be contractive. The same holds in the commutative category.

For general Banach algebras without additional structure, it is folklore that there exists no separable commutative Banach algebra $B$ such that every separable commutative Banach algebra admits an \emph{isometric} embedding into $B$. A straightforward proof appears in \cite{Kania:2012}: if $p$ and $q$ are commuting projections in a Banach algebra, then $\|p - q\| \ge 1$, so any set of commuting projections is discrete; since projections in Banach algebras can have arbitrarily large norms, a commutative Banach algebra containing uncountably many commuting projections must be non-separable.

We work over \(\C\) however the results remain valid for real Banach algebras too. A \emph{homomorphism} between Banach algebras means a bounded algebra homomorphism. An \emph{embedding} is an injective homomorphism that is a topological isomorphism onto its closed range.

We present a single proof that covers \emph{all four} cases. Contractivity plays no r\^{o}le beyond boundedness.

We refer to \cite[Chapter~2]{Ryan:2002} for all facts related to the projective tensor product of Banach spaces and to \cite[Chapter 2]{Dales:2000} for Banach-algebraic aspects thereof. For a Banach space \(X\) and an integer \(n\), the (\(n\)-fold) \emph{projective tensor product}
\(X^{\widehat{\otimes}_{\pi} n}\) (or simply \(X\widehat{\otimes}_{\pi} X\) for \(n=2\)) is defined as the completion of the
algebraic tensor product \(X^{\otimes n}\) under the projective norm.
\begin{theorem}[Universality of the projective tensor product]\label{thm:proj-universal}
Let \(X,Y\) be Banach spaces and \(n\in\mathbb{N}\). For every continuous \(n\)-linear map
\(M\colon X^{n}\to Y\) there exists a unique bounded operator
\(L_{M}\in\mathcal{L}\!\big(X^{\widehat{\otimes}_{\pi} n},\,Y\big)\) such that
\[
M=L_{M}\circ\otimes^{n}_{\pi}.
\]
Moreover, the correspondence \(M\mapsto L_{M}\) is an isometric linear isomorphism
\[
\mathcal{L}(X^{n};Y)\ \cong\ \mathcal{L}\!\big(X^{\widehat{\otimes}_{\pi} n},\,Y\big),
\]
so that \(\|L_{M}\|=\|M\|\).
Diagrammatically,
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large, row sep=large]
& X^{n} \arrow[dl, swap, "\otimes^{n}_{\pi}"] \arrow[dr, "M"] & \\
X^{\widehat{\otimes}_{\pi} n} \arrow[rr, swap, "L_{M}"] && Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

We include the following well-known result for the sake of completeness in order to identify the radical of the test algebras we are about to construct, even though irrelevant for the proof of the main theorem.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:nil-in-rad}
Let $A$ be a Banach algebra and let $I\triangleleft A$ be a (two‑sided)
ideal with $I^{n}=0$ for some $n\in\mathbb{N}$. Then $I\subseteq\rad(A)$.
Moreover, if $A/I$ is semisimple (equivalently $\rad(A/I)=0$), then
$\rad(A)\subseteq I$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lifting-unified}
Let $G$ be a separable Banach space, and let $U,Y$ be Banach spaces.
Suppose $S:U\to Y$ is a compact operator which does not factor through $G$.
Set $X:=U\oplus_{1}\C$ and define the symmetric bilinear map
\[
\beta_{U}\big((u,\lambda),(u',\lambda')\big):=\lambda u'+\lambda' u.
\]
Let $E:=X\otpi X$ and let $\widetilde{\beta}_{U}:E\to U$ be the (unsymmetric) linearisation of $\beta_{U}$.
Then $\widetilde{\beta}_{U}$ is surjective and admits a bounded right inverse $\sigma:U\to E$ given by
$\sigma(u):=(u,0)\otimes(0,1)$. Consequently, for $T_{0}:=S\circ \widetilde{\beta}_{U}:E\to Y$ we have:
\begin{itemize}
\item $T_{0}$ is compact; and
\item $T_{0}$ does not factor through $G$ (otherwise $S=T_{0}\circ\sigma$ would factor through $G$).
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

The contradiction implies that no such embedding \(\phi:A \to B\) can exist.
\begin{itemize}
 \item In cases \textup{(A)} and \textup{(B)}, we assumed \(B\) was a universal separable \emph{commutative} Banach algebra. We have constructed a separable commutative Banach algebra \(A\) that does not embed into \(B\), a contradiction.
 \item In cases \textup{(C)} and \textup{(D)}, we assumed \(B\) was a universal separable Banach algebra for \emph{all} separable Banach algebras. We have constructed a specific separable (and, as it happens, commutative) Banach algebra \(A\) that does not embed into \(B\), which again is a~contradiction.
\end{itemize}
Since our choice of \(B\) was arbitrary, no such universal object exists in any of the specified categories. The argument relied only on the boundedness of the embedding, so it holds equally for contractive embeddings. This completes the proof of \textup{(A)}–\textup{(D)}.
\end{proof}

